# kaspa-vault configuration — copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# `.env` is read from the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY, not from where the binary
# lives. Override with `--env-file PATH`. kaspa-vault prints the absolute path
# of whichever file it loaded.
#
# This file holds key material in PLAINTEXT. kaspa-vault refuses to read a
# `.env` that other users can read, so keep it at mode 600. `.env` is in
# .gitignore; do not commit it, and keep it out of backups you do not control.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key material — supply exactly ONE of the following.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A BIP39 mnemonic phrase. Fully hardened derivation, isolated from any
# classical Kaspa account under the same seed. This is the recommended form.
#KASPA_VAULT_MNEMONIC="abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about"

# Or a key, in any of three formats (auto-detected):
#
#   xprv... / kprv...   extended private key, with its chain code
#   128 hex characters  a BIP39 seed
#   64  hex characters  a bare private key
#
# WARNING on the bare form: a 32-byte key has no chain code, so it cannot be
# hardened-isolated. If it is or ever was a Kaspa spending key, its public key
# is already on-chain — and a quantum adversary who recovers it can derive this
# vault too, leaving no post-quantum protection at all. Only use a key
# generated solely for this purpose.
#
# An extended private key is safe if it has never been exported. If its
# matching xpub was shared with a watch-only wallet, an exchange, or accounting
# software, that branch is recoverable and so is everything beneath it.
#KASPA_VAULT_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-scheme overrides — optional.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The derivation path is m/101110'/111111'/scheme'/account'/index', so one
# mnemonic already yields an independent vault per signature scheme. That is
# the intended shape: one seed, one backup phrase, every scheme under it. Leave
# these unset and SLH-DSA derives from KASPA_VAULT_MNEMONIC like everything
# else.
#
# Set them to point a scheme at a *different* seed. The reason to do that is
# testing: a scheme whose key derivation has not yet been exercised on-chain
# should not be pointed at the seed that already holds funds. Whichever
# variable an address came from is printed alongside it, so there is never any
# doubt about which phrase restores it.
#
#KASPA_VAULT_MNEMONIC_SLH="..."
#KASPA_VAULT_KEY_SLH=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults — both optional. Command-line flags override these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# tn10 (default) or mainnet.
#KASPA_VAULT_NETWORK=tn10

# Which vault under the seed. Each key index is an independent vault with its
# own 32,768 one-time keys; deriving one takes a few seconds.
#KASPA_VAULT_KEY_INDEX=0
